(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the core architectural difference between mutable and immutable data structures in Python with memory diagrams.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   In Python, every entity is an object stored in heap memory and referenced by variable) Tj
T*
(   identifiers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Immutable Objects \(int, float, str, tuple, frozenset, bytes\):) Tj
T*
(   Once instantiated, their internal state and memory contents cannot be altered. Any operation) Tj
T*
(   that appears to modify an immutable object actually allocates a new object in memory with a) Tj
T*
(   distinct `id\(\)` and updates the reference.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mutable Objects \(list, dict, set, bytearray\):) Tj
T*
(   These structures can modify their internal contents in-place without changing their memory) Tj
T*
(   address `id\(\)`. When an element is added to a list or a key-value pair is inserted into a) Tj
T*
(   dictionary, the container mutates its internal pointer array, preserving identity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Illustrate the nuances of string slicing in Python, including step parameters, negative indexing, and boundary conditions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   String slicing follows the generalized syntax: `sequence[start:stop:step]`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   - Default start is 0, default stop is length of string, and default step is 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Negative indices count backwards from the end \(-1 is the last character\).) Tj
T*
(   - A negative step reverses traversal direction. For example, `'ABCDEF'[::-1]` reverses the) Tj
T*
(   entire string.) Tj
T*
(   - Boundary conditions: When slicing, Python never raises `IndexError` even if indices are out) Tj
T*
(   of bounds; it gracefully clamps indices to the sequence boundaries.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 3:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare `list.append\(\)`, `list.extend\(\)`, and list concatenation `+` in terms of memory allocation and behavior.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Comparison of list insertion mechanisms:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. `append\(object\)`:) Tj
T*
(   Adds its exact argument as a single element to the end of the list. If a list is appended, it) Tj
T*
(   becomes an inner nested list: `[1, 2].append\([3, 4]\) -> [1, 2, [3, 4]]`. Modifies in-place,) Tj
T*
(   amortized O\(1\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. `extend\(iterable\)`:) Tj
T*
(   Iterates over the given iterable and appends each element individually to the end. `[1,) Tj
T*
(   2].extend\([3, 4]\) -> [1, 2, 3, 4]`. Modifies in-place, O\(k\) where k is iterable length.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Concatenation `+`:) Tj
T*
(   Allocates an entirely new list containing all elements of both operands. Does not modify) Tj
T*
(   original lists. Requires O\(n + m\) time and memory.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
