(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the time and space complexity of Python's TimSort algorithm used in `list.sort\(\)`.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   TimSort is a hybrid sorting algorithm derived from Merge Sort and Insertion Sort, designed by) Tj
T*
(   Tim Peters in 2002.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Algorithm Mechanics:) Tj
T*
(   1. It scans the array for natural ordered sequences called 'runs' \(either strictly ascending or) Tj
T*
(   strictly descending\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Short runs are extended to a minimum length \(MINRUN, typically 32-64\) using Binary Insertion) Tj
T*
(   Sort.) Tj
T*
(   3. Runs are pushed to a stack and merged according to strict balancing invariants: A > B + C) Tj
T*
(   and B > C.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Complexity Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   - Best-Case Time: O\(N\) when the data is already sorted or nearly sorted, requiring only run) Tj
T*
(   detection.) Tj
T*
(   - Average and Worst-Case Time: O\(N log N\) with small constant factors.) Tj
T*
(   - Space Complexity: O\(N\) auxiliary memory for merge buffer buffers.) Tj
T*
(   - Stability: TimSort is completely stable \(maintains relative order of equal keys\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
