(   Each entry had `[hash \(8B\), key_ptr \(8B\), value_ptr \(8B\)]` = 24 bytes.) Tj
T*
(   For an 8-slot table with 3 entries, 5 slots remained empty, wasting 5 * 24 = 120 bytes of RAM,) Tj
T*
(   and insertion order was lost.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Python 3.6+ \(Compact & Ordered Dict\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Divided into two arrays:) Tj
T*
(   1. Sparse Indices Table: An array of small signed integers \(int8, int16, etc.\) mapping hash) Tj
T*
(   buckets to indices.) Tj
T*
(   2. Dense Entries Array: A packed array containing `[hash, key, val]` stored in strict) Tj
T*
(   chronological insertion order.) Tj
T*
(   Benefits: Saves 30% to 95% memory overhead and natively preserves dictionary insertion order) Tj
T*
(   without performance penalties.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
